title: Codex app-server startup fails on slow readiness and reports misleading upgrade guidance
updated_at: "2026-07-14T08:41:37Z"
---
Observed when running the local cx alias:

    cx --model gpt-5.6-luna

Output:

    [c2c codex app-server] startup failed: app-server exited before becoming ready
      codex version codex-cli 0.144.1; minimum supported for app-server mode is 0.144.0.
      falling back to the hook-backed Codex launch automatically. To get
      arrival-time app-server delivery, upgrade codex (>= 0.144.0) and relaunch.
      diagnostic: {"error":"codex_app_server_launch_failed","code":"server_died_before_ready","message":"app-server exited before becoming ready","codex_version":"codex-cli 0.144.1","min_codex_version":"0.144.0"}
    [c2c-start/codex-polar-meadow-hogo] iter 1: launching codex (outer pid=3702241)
    [c2c-start] launch: /home/xertrov/.bun/bin/codex resume --last --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox --model gpt-5.6-luna

The installed Codex version is already >= the stated minimum, so telling the operator to upgrade is incorrect for this failure. Investigate whether app-server readiness is timing out or being classified as server_died_before_ready during slow startup (model selection, auth, resource contention, or cold start).

Desired behavior:
- distinguish an actual early process exit from a readiness timeout;
- give an actionable diagnostic that reflects the real failure;
- tolerate legitimately slow app-server startup where safe (for example, configurable or appropriately increased readiness deadline);
- preserve automatic hook-backed fallback;
- add regression coverage for slow startup and for codex >= minimum version.

Additional operator-facing issue:

When app-server startup fails and c2c falls back to the hook-backed launch, it currently resumes the most recent Codex session (`resume --last`). This was confusing and may attach the new invocation to unrelated prior work. Do not resume the most recent session as the failure fallback; start a fresh session or make the session-selection behavior explicit and intentional.